    49	| Investment commitments | **Finding / application risk:** funded cash can be read as a part of the original commitments, while the remaining balance and changes are other figures. The inserted rule pushes whichever amount the model treats as the whole over the part, although the outcome names all three and does not specify that priority. It does not reliably identify a single whole, so the particular winner remains ambiguous. |
    50	| Memory prices | **Not clearly decided.** Contract-price changes, makers’ selling prices, and gross margins are distinct measures. The last sentence calls separate figures “several things,” but does not say which is main. If a model treats a reported price as a part or condition of the general price figure, the broad clause creates a risk of an unsupported choice; that relationship is not explicit. |
    51	| Chip sales into China | **Not decided by the literal figure rule.** US approval and Chinese acceptance are events or authorities’ decisions, not a figure and its part. |
    52	| Acquisition | **Not decided by the literal figure rule.** Signing and closing are events; consideration and conditions accompany them. The edit gives no event-selection rule. |
    53	| Chip export licence | **Not decided by the literal figure rule.** Licence status and resumed revenue are different kinds of outcome; the edit does not say which is main. |
    54	
    55	The boundary is consequently not protected: the optical case at least is exposed to an automatic choice, and commitments are exposed to the same broad reading. [D631](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8534>) says D436 keeps the sides of an **identified** bet together; it does not give a general rule for identifying the main part.
    56	
    57	5. **The examples**
    58	
    59	- **Bank loans / commercial share:** Mirrors the total-versus-portion structure of `acc/clean-9` in the [44 test topics](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/cases.json>). It also resembles `pool-17`, though that target is outside those 44. It uses a banking measure, but the whole-and-share idea transfers to all four industries. Its orientation matches D436’s chip-total example when the total is the tracked bet.
    60	- **Carmaker deliveries / electric share:** Also mirrors `acc/clean-9`’s total-and-portion structure, and closely mirrors `pool-17`’s shipments and composition. It transfers across industries as a total and product mix. Its orientation matches D436’s chip-total example in that setting.
    61	- **Airline passengers / planes staying full:** Mirrors the supply-versus-demand relationship in `acc/clean-8` and the capacity-keeping-pace relationship in `acc/clean-10`, both among the 44. The concepts transfer, though “planes stay full” is airline-specific. **Its orientation conflicts with D436’s third example:** it chooses demand over a capacity-utilization side only if passenger count is treated as demand; the wording “planes stay full” instead measures demand relative to capacity. It is an imperfect analogy and does not teach the distinct D436 case where **capacity keeps pace with demand**. It cannot support a general rule that capacity or the first named figure always wins.
    62	- **Retail sales / shelves running short:** Mirrors `acc/clean-8`’s shipments-and-shortage structure in the 44. The supply-shortage relationship transfers to the other industries, although shelves are retail-specific. Its orientation is consistent with D436’s shipment-and-shortage example.
    63	
    64	These are changed-industry examples rather than copied company names or numbers. Nevertheless, they mirror the test topics’ structures closely, and the two total-and-share examples repeat essentially the same classification. They do not establish a closed, sourced boundary for when related measures are one bet. The example and generality requirements are in [AGENTS.md §6](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:194>).
    65	
    66	6. **The last sentence**
    67	
    68	“Two separate figures, or one figure for two years, are still several things” is **a new general rule as written**. The existing request supports one figure for one named period, and D397 supports declining when several things are tracked with no main one. Neither says every pair of separate figures is necessarily a separate bet: D436 explicitly keeps capacity keeping pace beside demand as context. Nor does a comparison with an earlier year by itself establish two competing tracked annual outcomes.
    69	
