FILE narrow-d436/audit-v2-codex.md
     1	**NOT SOUND.** The passage correctly points toward judging shipments, the total, or demand in the examples Alex named. But it turns a resemblance in wording into an automatic decision about which figure is the main question. For example, it can treat any figure mentioned beside a supply shortage as the main figure, even when the outcome does not establish that relationship.
     2	
     3	This is a read-only assessment of what the text instructs; it is not a model test. The inserted passage is in [edited-system.txt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/edited-system.txt:5).
     4	
     5	### 1. Clause by clause
     6	
     7	| Passage | Finding |
     8	|---|---|
     9	| “When the outcome names a figure together with the share of it that one kind makes up” | **Keeps wider.** D436 identifies the AI-chip share beside the chip total as two sides of one bet. This sentence makes *every* total-and-share pairing one bet and selects the total, without first establishing that it is the same bet. D397 says to judge the main part named by the outcome; D631 and D635 do not establish a general way to choose between several parts. |
    10	| “or with whether supply stays short of demand or capacity keeps pace with it” | **Keeps wider.** D436 names shortage beside shipments and capacity keeping pace beside demand. The passage attaches either condition to an unspecified “figure.” The grammar does not require that the figure be shipments or demand, or even that it be the quantity to which the condition relates. |
    11	| “the two are one bet seen from two sides, not several things with none the main one” | **Keeps wider and conflicts with the refusal rule.** D436 describes an identified bet; this sentence declares the named shapes to be one bet. It overrides [system line 6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/edited-system.txt:6) and the schema’s `no_ladder` description where the outcome genuinely has several parts and no main one. |
    12	| “ask the figure” | **Keeps wider.** It selects the figure even when the outcome does not establish that it is the tracked part. That can conflict with “Judge the one the topic’s outcome names as what is tracked” and with the closing check’s “the one the topic’s outcome names.” It also does not distinguish demand from capacity when the latter is the figure. |
    13	| “list the share or the shortage in not_judged” | **Consistent but incomplete.** Showing the other side as not judged agrees with D436 and D397. The sentence does not explicitly name capacity keeping pace as something to list, although the preceding general instruction and the closing check require every omitted part to be listed. D436 also says the second side stays as context; the insertion only says to list it. |
    14	| Bank, retailer and utility examples | **The stated orientations match the three D436 examples**, but the examples repeat the broad automatic selection above. The utility example correctly asks demand and leaves capacity as the other side. |
    15	
    16	The `no_ladder` description and closing check remain unchanged, so the request now has competing instructions in cases where a surface match does not establish a main part. Other refusal grounds, such as an unavailable publication date, remain in force; the added sentence does not expressly cancel them.
    17	
